# Generate test functions from validated manifest (macro-based)

## Context
We want each extension to be its own #[test] function for:
- Parallelism (cargo test runs tests in parallel)
- Isolation (one failure does not block others)
- Clear reporting (each extension shows as pass/fail in test output)

## Approach
Similar to fixture_test! macro in conformance_fixtures.rs:

macro_rules! conformance_test {
    ($name:ident, $ext_id:literal) => {
        #[test]
        fn $name() {
            let result = run_conformance_test($ext_id);
            assert!(result.status == "pass", "Extension {} failed: {:?}", $ext_id, result.diffs);
        }
    };
}

conformance_test!(conformance_hello, "hello");
conformance_test!(conformance_pirate, "pirate");
// ... generated for all validated extensions

## Build Script Alternative
Could use a build.rs that reads VALIDATED_MANIFEST.json and generates test functions. This avoids manually maintaining the list. Tradeoff: build.rs adds complexity but eliminates manual maintenance.

## Decision
Start with manual macro invocations for the first 60 official extensions. Add build.rs generation later when corpus is larger.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Each official pi-mono extension has a #[test] function
- Tests can be filtered: cargo test conformance_hello
- Tests can be run by tier: cargo test conformance_tier1

# Update --continue flag to use SQLite index

## Goal
Use SQLite index for fast lookup of most recent session when --continue is used.

## Current Implementation (src/main.rs)
Currently scans filesystem for most recent:
```rust
if args.continue_session {
    let session_dir = sessions_dir_for_cwd(&cwd);
    // Scans all files, sorts by mtime
    let most_recent = fs::read_dir(&session_dir)?
        .filter_map(|e| e.ok())
        .max_by_key(|e| e.metadata().ok()?.modified().ok()?);
    // ...
}
```

## New Implementation
```rust
if args.continue_session {
    let index = SessionIndex::open_default()?;
    let cwd = std::env::current_dir()?.to_string_lossy().to_string();

    if let Some(meta) = index.find_recent(&cwd)? {
        return load_session(&meta.path);
    }

    // Fallback to filesystem
    // ...
}
```

## Benefits
- O(1) lookup instead of O(n) filesystem scan
- Works across hundreds of sessions instantly
- Consistent with session picker behavior

## Dependencies
- bd-3nz (indexing must work first)

## Testing
- Test: --continue uses index
- Test: --continue falls back to filesystem
- Test: Performance improved

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] --continue queries index
- [ ] Fallback works if index empty
- [ ] Faster for users with many sessions

# Create standard event payload fixtures for both runtimes

## Context
Extensions subscribe to events via pi.on("event_name", handler). For conformance testing, we need to FIRE the same events in both runtimes and compare handler responses.

## Events Defined in pi-mono types.ts
From the ExtensionEvent type union:
- tool_call: {tool, input} -> {allow, message, replace_input}
- tool_result: {tool, result} -> {result} (can modify)
- turn_start: {} -> void
- turn_end: {} -> void
- before_agent_start: {} -> {message, system_prompt}
- input: {content, source} -> {content, skip}
- context: {messages, usage} -> {system_prompt, messages}
- resources_discover: {resources} -> {resources}
- user_bash: {command, result} -> {modified_result}
- session_before_compact: {preparation} -> {should_compact, custom_messages}
- session_before_tree: {options} -> {should_show_tree}

## What To Do
1. For each event type, create 2-3 standard payloads (normal case + edge cases)
2. Save as tests/ext_conformance/fixtures/event_payloads.json
3. Document expected behavior for each event type
4. Include payloads that test: normal flow, blocking behavior, error handling, empty handlers

## Fixture Format
{
  "event_payloads": {
    "tool_call": [
      {
        "name": "basic_tool_call",
        "payload": {"tool": "bash", "input": {"command": "echo hi"}},
        "default_response": {"allow": true}
      },
      {
        "name": "blocked_tool_call",
        "payload": {"tool": "rm", "input": {"path": "/etc/passwd"}},
        "default_response": {"allow": false, "message": "blocked"}
      }
    ]
  }
}

## Acceptance Criteria
- All event types from ExtensionEvent have at least 2 payloads
- Payloads are valid JSON matching the TypeScript type definitions
- Edge cases included (empty arrays, null values, unicode, large payloads)

# Task: Implement Tool Hostcall Handler

## Objective

Implement the handler for HostcallKind::Tool that routes pi.tool() calls from extensions to the Rust ToolRegistry.

## Background

When an extension calls pi.tool("read", {path: "foo.txt"}), the flow is:
1. JS: pi.tool() enqueues HostcallRequest with kind=Tool
2. Rust: Dispatcher drains request, extracts tool name + params
3. Rust: Looks up tool in ToolRegistry, calls execute()
4. Rust: Converts ToolOutput to HostcallOutcome
5. JS: Promise resolves with result

## Implementation

```rust
impl ExtensionDispatcher {
    /// Handle pi.tool(name, input) hostcalls.
    /// 
    /// This allows extensions to invoke any tool in the registry,
    /// including built-in tools and other extension-registered tools.
    async fn dispatch_tool(
        &self,
        name: &str,
        input: serde_json::Value,
    ) -> HostcallOutcome {
        // Find tool in registry
        let Some(tool) = self.tool_registry.get(name) else {
            return HostcallOutcome::Error {
                code: "TOOL_NOT_FOUND".to_string(),
                message: format!("Tool '{}' not found in registry", name),
            };
        };

        // Generate unique tool_call_id for tracking
        let tool_call_id = format!("ext-{}", uuid::Uuid::new_v4().to_string()[..8].to_string());

        // Execute the tool
        match tool.execute(&tool_call_id, input, None).await {
            Ok(output) => {
                // Convert ToolOutput to JSON Value
                let result = serde_json::json!({
                    "content": output.content,
                    "details": output.details,
                });
                HostcallOutcome::Success(result)
            }
            Err(e) => HostcallOutcome::Error {
                code: "TOOL_EXECUTION_ERROR".to_string(),
                message: e.to_string(),
            },
        }
    }
}
```

## Error Handling

| Error Condition | Code | Behavior |
|-----------------|------|----------|
| Tool not found | TOOL_NOT_FOUND | Return error outcome |
| Tool execution fails | TOOL_EXECUTION_ERROR | Return error with message |
| Invalid params | INVALID_PARAMS | Return validation error |
| Timeout | TIMEOUT | Return timeout error |

## Integration with Update Callbacks

The TypeScript reference supports streaming tool updates via the onUpdate callback. For v1, we can omit this (return final result only). Future enhancement:

```rust
// Future: Support streaming updates
let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(16);
let update_callback = Some(Box::new(move |update| {
    let _ = tx.try_send(update);
}));
```

## Testing

1. Unit test: Tool found and executed successfully
2. Unit test: Tool not found returns error
3. Unit test: Tool execution error propagates
4. Integration test: Built-in tool (read) via extension
5. Integration test: Extension-registered tool via another extension

## Dependencies

- Depends on: bd-389q (ExtensionDispatcher struct)
- Blocks: End-to-end extension tests

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] dispatch_tool() method implemented
- [ ] Tool lookup in registry works
- [ ] Successful execution returns Success outcome
- [ ] Errors return Error outcome with code + message
- [ ] Tool call IDs are unique
- [ ] Unit tests pass

# Update session picker to use SQLite index

## Goal
Replace filesystem walking in session picker with fast SQLite index queries.

## Current Implementation (src/session_picker.rs)
Currently scans filesystem:
```rust
// Slow: walks all session directories
let sessions = walkdir::WalkDir::new(&sessions_dir)
    .into_iter()
    .filter_map(|e| e.ok())
    .filter(|e| e.path().extension() == Some("jsonl"))
    .collect();
```

## New Implementation
```rust
use crate::session_index::SessionIndex;

impl SessionPicker {
    pub fn load_sessions(cwd: Option<&str>) -> Result<Vec<SessionMeta>> {
        let index = SessionIndex::open_default()?;
        index.list_sessions(cwd)
    }
}
```

## UI Changes
- Display indexed metadata directly (no file reads needed)
- Show message count from index
- Show session name from index
- Faster render (no filesystem I/O)

## Fallback Behavior
If index unavailable or empty:
```rust
fn load_sessions_with_fallback(cwd: Option<&str>) -> Result<Vec<SessionMeta>> {
    // Try index first
    if let Ok(index) = SessionIndex::open_default() {
        if let Ok(sessions) = index.list_sessions(cwd) {
            if !sessions.is_empty() {
                return Ok(sessions);
            }
        }
    }

    // Fallback to filesystem (and populate index)
    let sessions = load_sessions_from_filesystem(cwd)?;
    // Optionally trigger reindex in background
    Ok(sessions)
}
```

## Dependencies
- bd-3nz (indexing must work first)

## Testing
- Test: Session picker shows indexed sessions
- Test: Picker falls back to filesystem if needed
- Test: Performance improved (measure time)

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Picker queries index by default
- [ ] Fallback to filesystem works
- [ ] UI displays index metadata
- [ ] Noticeably faster for 50+ sessions

